  "title": "Pointers and Arrays",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c14-l1",
      "title": "Arrays Are Addresses",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "The name of an array, used on its own, is the address of its first element: a is the same as &a[0]. So int *p = a; makes p point to the start of the array.",
        "That's why functions get arrays as pointers: void show(int *a, int n) and void show(int a[], int n) mean exactly the same thing. The array itself is never copied.",
        "And it's why a function can change the caller's array, while it can't change the caller's plain int."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "void double_all(int *a, int n) {",
          "    int i;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)",
          "        a[i] = a[i] * 2;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int v[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};",
          "    int i;",
          "    double_all(v, 4);",
          "    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)",
          "        printf(\"%d \", v[i]);",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "2 4 6 8 "
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
