    {
      "id": "c14-l2",
      "title": "Pointer Arithmetic",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Adding to a pointer moves it by whole elements: if p points to a[0], then p + 1 points to a[1], and *(p + 2) is a[2]. In fact, C defines a[i] as *(a + i).",
        "p++ moves the pointer to the next element. Many classic C loops walk through an array this way instead of using an index.",
        "Subtracting two pointers into the same array gives the number of elements between them."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};",
          "    int *p = a;",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p);",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", *(p + 2));",
          "    p++;",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", *p);",
          "    printf(\"%d apart\\n\", (int) (&a[4] - p));",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "10",
          "30",
          "20",
          "3 apart"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
