    {
      "id": "c14-l3",
      "title": "Strings as char *",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A string constant like \"unix\" is an array somewhere in memory, and char *s = \"unix\"; points to its first letter. Walking through it with s++ until *s is '\\0' is classic C.",
        "while (*s) means \"while the character isn't '\\0'\", because '\\0' is 0, which is false.",
        "Kernighan and Ritchie's famous strcpy is just one line: while ((*dst++ = *src++) != '\\0'); Short, fast and hard to read: very 1970s."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int length(char *s) {",
          "    int n = 0;",
          "    while (*s) {",
          "        n++;",
          "        s++;",
          "    }",
          "    return n;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char *msg = \"hello, unix\";",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", length(msg));",
          "    while (*msg)",
          "        putchar(*msg++);",
          "    putchar('\\n');",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "11",
          "hello, unix"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c14-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
